0 pts) and Hall (180 mins, no availability flag). Calafiori stays on superior form. (2) Fernandez-Pardo arrives as likely Newcastle first-choice striker, conf 0.8, minutes unproven, so Wissa is last on the bench. Calvert-Lewin: no signal, certain minutes, weakest form, benched for a 4-5-1 of five certain-minute midfielders. Regression flags on Egan, Tarkowski, Hall and Stach are xG debate, outside my diet: ignored. No strong positive signal on any starter, so captain the most secure starter by minutes and points: B.Fernandes (180, 25); vice João Pedro (180, 20), brief says hold. Quiet week, no chip.","bounced":[],"rollover":false}],"draft_rationale":"No brief available. With the wire silent there is no information edge to trade on, so this draft optimises the one thing my charter lets me use without signals: expected minutes, with recent form as secondary confirmation. Every player has 170+ of a possible 180 minutes over the last two gameweeks (Wissa 171 the lowest), so the XI and bench are ordered purely by certainty of starting. Fernandes (25 pts, 180 min) is the most reliable captain by minutes and points; Palmer and João Pedro follow. Cheap full-minute starters (Tzolakis, Egan, Stach, Groß, Lewis-Potter) fund the premiums without rotation risk. I leave 4.9m in the bank deliberately: when the News Brain goes live, the first high-confidence availability signal must be actionable within one deadline, and dry powder means I can buy the best-minutes replacement rather than the cheapest. Fixtures, ownership and price trends were ignored per my diet.","charter":{"philosophy":"Markets move on information, and FPL's market is slow. A press-conference hint, an injury in training, a benching pattern — these are worth points to whoever acts first. I read the intelligence brief the way a trader reads a wire feed: I act on high-confidence signals within one deadline, before ownership and price catch up, and I hold still when the wire is quiet.","objective":"Maximise points ca